Keys and locked doors might be more like real life:
- most keys open only one door, chest etc.
- most doors are locked only from the external side.
- rarely, a master key opens several locks that form a meaningful set
(e.g. all cells in one corridor of a prison, all lockers in a swimming
pool, etc.).
- even more rarely, a lock requires multiple keys held by different
people.
- people have the keys of locks of things and places they own (e.g.
their home, their car) or that they are entitled to open and close
(e.g. safes and vaults for a bank manager, an office complex for a
trusted employee); knowing the origin of a stolen key is a very good
indication of what it could open.
- for each lock there are often several copies of the key, assigned to
each eligible user.
- keys are usually held by people; when they are not, they are locked
into a safe place (requiring other keys) where only trusted people can
get them.
- there are easily distinguishable key classes with different shapes
and sizes, but individual key shapes are very hard to recognize;
comparing two keys requires holding both at the same time.
- by looking at a lock, most types of key classes can be excluded.
- there so many possible keys of any class that trying to open a lock
with the apparently compatible unrelated keys from the keyring is
futile (but it can be a desperate measure).
- trying key after key is usually -fast, but not enough to do it during
combat.
- without appropriate tools and (usually) a lot of patience and
strength, smashing most doors is difficult.
- picking locks requires specialized tools and training, but very
little talent.
- there are strong disincentives to destroying or picking locks (and to
theft and trespassing in general); having lockpicking tools, crowbars
etc. is a serious liability by itself.
- a broken door that cannot be locked can be a serious problem

If we see these obvious features of real world locks from the
malevolent point of view of a typical RL protagonist out to kill
everyone and stuff everything into his backpack, there is an obvious
cost and benefit structure:
1) Locked doors are a major obstacle; most locks are too difficult,
risky and/or unrewarding to be attacked casually, but within the
possibilities of a motivated and resourceful person. For example,
generic burglary in an Angband-style village should be nothing more
than a way to get arrested and attacked, but entering the king's
treasure vault can be a quest (or an optional but common plan to turn
time, food etc. into treasure), with different approaches (hunting the
chief of the palace guards to get the key, use a magical wall or some
allies to remain undisturbed while picking the lock, acquire
teleportation magic, etc.).
2)The easiest way to open a door, chest etc. is obtaining its key from
one of the easily identifiable legitimate key owners. Looking out for
the holders of particularly important keys can be very important.
Monsters of most intelligent and/or humanoid types could have a home
behind a locked door of which they have the key, which can be stolen or
looted to provide access to a more or less rewarding amount of treasure
and information. Players could notice where monsters come from, to look
for their homes, choose to kill monsters in dangerous, slow and
inefficient ways to avoid losing or destroying keys and decide whether
finding and looting yet another house is useful.
3) Being unable to close a smashed or destructively unlocked door can
be very bad.
This cost can be increased with large numbers of systematically
wandering monsters and some not too dumb pathfinding (for example, if a
monster repeatedly makes a tour that brings it in sight of every
reachable square it is guaranteed to come through any newly opened
door).
4) Unidentified keys are useless.
On the other hand there is magical identification: there can be vague
answers like "this key opens a door in level 5 of the Tower" or "this
key opens the private storeroom of high wizard Foo" or more generous
ones, like "this key opens the private storeroom of high wizard Foo,
which can be found in level 5 of the Tower and is believed to contain a
trophy collection of magical rings displayed on their original severed
fingers".
Of course the expensive result can be "this key used to open a large
reinforced wooden chest on level 4 that was destroyed 1234 turns ago by
an explosive trap triggered by your overconfident lockpicking attempt"

